course: dictionary
lesson: 4456
title: Happiness: a noun for a feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Happiness is a noun. A noun names a person, place, thing or idea, and feelings are ideas you cannot touch. «Crying at music, from happiness or sadness, is normal.» Here happiness names a feeling, just as sadness does in the same sentence.
explain: Look at the word family: happy, happily, happiness. Happy describes a person: «She is happy.» Happiness names the feeling itself: «She filled the room with happiness.» Notice that the y in happy changes to i before -ness.
explain: Happiness can also mean a good life full of joy and contentment. «They live in happiness» means their whole life feels good, not just one happy moment. To check you have a noun, try putting «the» or «some» in front of it: «some happiness» works, so it is a noun.
fact: Happiness is a noun: it names a feeling, which is an idea you cannot touch.
fact: Happiness means the feeling of being happy, glad and pleased.
fact: Happiness can also mean a state of having a good life with joy and contentment.
fact: Happiness is one of the basic feelings, along with sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust.
fact: Happiness is uncountable, so we say «some happiness», not «two happinesses».
exercise: «She filled the room with happiness.» What kind of word is happiness here? | An adverb, describing how she moved | *A noun, naming a feeling | A verb, an action word | An adjective, describing a person
exercise: Which word describes a person, rather than naming a feeling? | Fear | Happiness | *Happy | Anger
exercise: Which spelling is correct for the noun form of happy? | *Happiness | Happiyness | Happyness | Happinness
test: «Crying at music, from happiness or sadness, is normal.» In this sentence, happiness is what kind of word? | An adjective | A verb | A preposition | *A noun
test: Which sentence uses happiness as a noun? | She happily waved at us. | *They lived in happiness for many years. | He was happy to see her. | The happy dog ran to the gate.
exam: Why is happiness a noun? | Because it links two words together | Because it describes how a person looks | Because it shows an action | *Because it names a feeling, an idea you cannot touch
